0241 | Let $M_n$ be a random $n \times n$ matrix with complex entries, which is not necessarily assumed to be Hermitian, and can be either a continuous or discrete ensemble of matrices. Then, counting multiplicities, there are $n$ complex (algebraic) eigenvalues, which we enumerate in an arbitrary fashion as
0242 | $$ \lambda_1(M_n), \dots, \lambda_n(M_n) \in \C.$$
0243 | One can then define, for each $1 \leq k \leq n$, the \emph{$k$-point correlation function}
0244 | $$\rho^{(k)}_n = \rho^{(k)}_n[M_n]: \C^k \to \R^+ $$  of the random matrix ensemble $M_n$ by requiring that
0245 | \begin{equation}\label{ck}
0246 | \begin{split}
0247 | & \int_{\C^k} F(z_1,\dots,z_k) \rho^{(k)}_n(z_1,\dots,z_k)\ dz_1 \dots dz_k  \\
0248 | &\quad = \E \sum_{1 \leq i_1 < \dots < i_k \leq n} F( \lambda_{i_1}(M_n), \dots, \lambda_{i_k}(M_n))
0249 | \end{split}
0250 | \end{equation}
