0605 | \section{Asymptotics for the complex gaussian ensemble}\label{kernel-app}
0606 | 
0607 | In this appendix we establish Lemma \ref{kernel}.  We begin with a computation of the asymptotics of the partial Taylor series
0608 | $$ e^{-z} \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} \frac{z^j}{j!}$$
0609 | for $z$ of size comparable to $n$.  We recall the \emph{error function}
0610 | $$ \erf(z) := \frac{2}{\sqrt{\pi}} \int_0^z e^{-t^2} dt$$
0611 | defined for all complex $z$, where the integral is over an arbitrary contour from $0$ to $z$.
0612 | 
0613 | \begin{lemma}[Taylor asymptotics]\label{taylor}  Let $n$ be a large integer (which we view as an asymptotic parameter going off to infinity), let $c \geq 0$ be a fixed real number, let $\alpha$ be a fixed complex number, and let $z$ be a complex number such that $z = cn + \alpha \sqrt{n} + o(\sqrt{n})$.
0614 | \begin{itemize}
0615 | \item[(i)]  If $c > 1$, then
0616 | \begin{equation}\label{easy}
0617 |  e^{-z} \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} \frac{z^j}{j!} = O(\exp(-\delta n))
0618 | \end{equation}
0619 | for some fixed $\delta>0$.
0620 | \item[(ii)]  If $c < 1$, then
0621 | \begin{equation}\label{easy-2}
0622 |  e^{-z} \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} \frac{z^j}{j!} = 1 - O(\exp(-\delta n)).
0623 | \end{equation}
0624 | for some fixed $\delta>0$.
0625 | \item[(iii)]  If $c=1$, then
0626 | $$ e^{-z} \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} \frac{z^j}{j!} = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2} \erf(-\sqrt{2} \alpha) + o(1).$$
0627 | \end{itemize}
0628 | The asymptotics in cases (i) and (ii) also hold when $c$ is no longer fixed, but is allowed to depend on $n$, so long as $c$ remains bounded, and also a bounded distance away from zero (thus $|c| \leq C$ and $||c|-1| \geq \eps$ for some fixed $C,\eps>0$).
0629 | \end{lemma}
0630 | 
0631 | \begin{proof}  We begin with the Taylor expansion
0632 | $$ e^{|z|} = \sum_{j=0}^\infty \frac{|z|^j}{j!}.$$
0633 | If $c > 1$, then by computing ratios we see that the terms $\frac{|z|^j}{j!}$ are exponentially increasing as $j$ ranges from $0$ to $(1+\eps) n$, for some fixed $\eps>0$.  As a consequence, we see that
0634 | $$ \frac{|z|^j}{j!} \leq e^{-\kappa n} e^{|z|}$$
0635 | for all $0 \leq j < n$ and some fixed $\kappa>0$.  Since $|z|-z = O(\sqrt{n})$, we obtain \eqref{easy} from the triangle inequality.
0636 | 
0637 | Similarly, if $c<1$, then $\frac{|z|^j}{j!}$ are exponentially decreasing as $j$ ranges from $(1-\eps) n$ to $\infty$ for some fixed $\eps>0$, and so
0638 | $$ \frac{|z|^j}{j!} \leq e^{-\kappa j} e^{|z|}$$
0639 | for all $j \geq n$ and some fixed $\kappa>0$.  In particular, we see that
0640 | $$ e^{-z} \sum_{j=n}^\infty \frac{z^j}{j!} = O(\exp(-\kappa n)).$$
0641 | Since
0642 | $$ e^{-z} \sum_{j=0}^\infty \frac{z^j}{j!} = 1,$$
0643 | the claim \eqref{easy-2} follows.
0644 | 
0645 | An inspection of the above arguments shows that the bounds continue to hold even when $c$ is allowed to vary in $n$, so long as it remains bounded and bounded away from zero; we leave the details to the reader.
0646 | 
0647 | Now suppose that $c=1$.  The arguments used to establish \eqref{easy} also establish that
0648 | $$ e^{-z} \sum_{j \leq (1-\eps) n} \frac{z^j}{j!} = o(1)$$
0649 | for any fixed $\eps>0$, and so we have
0650 | \begin{equation}\label{sas}
0651 | e^{-z} \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} \frac{z^j}{j!} = \sum_{n - f(n) \leq j < n} e^{-z} \frac{z^j}{j!} + o(1)
0652 | \end{equation}
0653 | whenever $f(n) = o(n)$ is such that $f(n)/n$ decays to zero sufficiently slowly as $n \to \infty$.
0654 | 
0655 | We may thus restrict $j$ to the regime $j = n - o(n)$. For this range of $j$, we have Stirling's approximation
0656 | $$ j! = (1 + o(1)) \sqrt{2\pi n} j^j e^{-j}$$
0657 | and so
0658 | $$ e^{-z} \frac{z^j}{j!} = \frac{1 + o(1)}{\sqrt{2\pi n}} \frac{z^j}{j^j} e^{j-z}.$$
0659 | We rewrite the right-hand side as
0660 | $$ \frac{1 + o(1)}{\sqrt{2\pi n}} \exp\left( j (\log( 1 + \frac{z-j}{j} ) - \frac{z-j}{j}) \right).$$
0661 | From Taylor expansion we have
0662 | $$ j (\log( 1 + \frac{z-j}{j} ) - \frac{z-j}{j})  = - (1+o(1)) \frac{(z-j)^2}{2j} = -(1+o(1))\frac{(n+\alpha \sqrt{n}-j)^2}{2n},$$
