0720 | \section{Asymptotics for the real gaussian ensemble}\label{real-app}
0721 | 
0722 | The purpose of this appendix is to establish Lemma \ref{corf}.  By reflection we may restrict attention to the case when $z_1,\ldots,z_l$ lie in the upper half-plane $\C_+$.  Our starting point is the explicit formula
0723 | $$ \rho^{(k,l)}_n(x_1,\ldots,x_k,z_1,\ldots,z_l) = \operatorname{Pf}( \begin{pmatrix} \tilde K_n(x_i,x_{i'}) & \tilde K_n(x_i,z_{j'}) \\
0724 | \tilde K_n(z_j, x_{i'}) & \tilde K_n(z_j, z_{j'}) )_{1 \leq i,i' \leq k; 1 \leq j,j' \leq l} \end{pmatrix}$$
0725 | for the correlation functions, where $\tilde K_n: (\R \cup \C_+) \times (\R \cup \C_+) \to M_2(\C)$ is a certain explicit $2 \times 2$ matrix kernel obeying the anti-symmetry law
0726 | \begin{equation}\label{kt}
0727 |  \tilde K(\zeta, \zeta') = -\tilde K(\zeta',\zeta)^T,
0728 | \end{equation}
0729 | making the expression inside the Pfaffian $\operatorname{Pf}$ an anti-symmetric $2(k+l) \times 2(k+l)$ matrix; see \cite[Theorem 8]{borodin}.  In view of this formula, we see that Lemma \ref{corf} will follow if we can establish the uniform bound
0730 | $$ \tilde K_n(\zeta,\zeta') = O(1)$$
0731 | for all $\zeta,\zeta' \in \R \cup \C_+$.
0732 | 
0733 | To do this, we will need the explicit description of the kernel $\tilde K_n$.  Following \cite{borodin}, we will need the partial cosine and exponential functions
0734 | \begin{align*}
0735 | c_{n/2}(\gamma) &:= \sum_{m=0}^{n/2-1}\frac{\gamma^{2m}}{(2m)!} \\
0736 | 
0737 | e_{n/2}(\gamma) &:= \sum_{m=0}^{n-2} \frac{\gamma^m}{m!}
0738 | \end{align*}
0739 | as well as the function
0740 | $$
0741 | r_{n/2}(z,x) := \frac{e^{-z^2/2}}{\sqrt{2\pi}} \sqrt{\operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{2}\Im z)} \frac{2^{(n-3)/2}}{(n-2)!} \sgn(x) z^{n-1} \gamma(\frac{n-1}{2},\frac{x^2}{2})
0742 | $$
0743 | where $\operatorname{erfc} := 1-\operatorname{erf}$ is the complementary error function and
0744 | $$
0745 | \gamma(t,x) = \int_0^x y^{t-1} e^{-y}\ dy
0746 | $$
0747 | is the incomplete gamma function.  In \cite[Theorem 8]{borodin}, the formula
0748 | $$ \tilde K_n(\gamma,\gamma') := \begin{pmatrix} \widetilde{DS}_n(\gamma,\gamma') & \widetilde {S}(\gamma,\gamma') \\ -\widetilde{S}(\gamma',\gamma) & \widetilde{ISM}_n(\gamma,\gamma') + {\mathcal E}(\gamma,\gamma') \end{pmatrix}
0749 | $$
0750 | is given for the kernel $\tilde K_n$,
0751 | where ${\mathcal E}(\gamma,\gamma')$ is equal to $\frac{1}{2} \sgn(\gamma-\gamma')$ when $\gamma,\gamma'$ are real, and equal to $0$ otherwise, and
0752 | the scalar quantities $\widetilde{DS}_n(\gamma,\gamma')$, $\widetilde {S}(\gamma,\gamma')$, $\widetilde{ISM}_n(\gamma,\gamma')$, are defined by the following formulae, depending on whether $\gamma,\gamma'$ are real or complex:
0753 | 
0754 | \begin{enumerate}
0755 | \item (Real-real case) If $x,x' \in \R$, then
0756 | \begin{align*}
0757 | \widetilde{S}_n(x,x') &:= \frac{e^{-(x-x')^2/2}}{\sqrt{2\pi}} e^{-xx'} e_{n/2}(xx') + r_{n/2}(x,x') \\
0758 | \widetilde{DS}_n(x,x') &:= \frac{e^{-(x-x')^2/2}}{\sqrt{2\pi}} (x'-x) e^{-xx'} e_{n/2}(xx') \\
0759 | \widetilde{IS}_n(x,x') &:= \frac{e^{-x^2/2}}{2\sqrt{\pi}} \sgn(x') \int_0^{(x')^2/2} \frac{e^{-t}}{\sqrt{t}} c_{n/2}(x\sqrt{2t})\ dt -
0760 | \frac{e^{-(x')^2/2}}{2\sqrt{\pi}} \sgn(x) \int_0^{x^2/2} \frac{e^{-t}}{\sqrt{t}} c_{n/2}(x'\sqrt{2t})\ dt.
0761 | \end{align*}
0762 | \item (Complex-complex case) If $z,z' \in \C_+$, then
0763 | \begin{align*}
0764 | \widetilde{S}_n(z,z') &:= \frac{i e^{-\frac{1}{2}(z-\overline{z'})^2}}{\sqrt{2\pi}} (\overline{z'} - z) \sqrt{\operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{2} \Im(z)) \operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{2} \Im(z'))} e^{-z\overline{z'}} e_{n/2}(z\overline{z'})\\
0765 | \widetilde{DS}_n(z,z') &:= \frac{e^{-\frac{1}{2}(z-z')^2}}{\sqrt{2\pi}} (z' - z) \sqrt{\operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{2} \Im(z)) \operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{2} \Im(z'))} e^{-z z'} e_{n/2}(z z') \\
0766 | \widetilde{IS}_n(z,z') &:= -\frac{e^{-\frac{1}{2}(\overline{z}-\overline{z'})^2}}{\sqrt{2\pi}} (\overline{z'} - \overline{z}) \sqrt{\operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{2} \Im(z)) \operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{2} \Im(z'))} e^{-\overline{z z'}} e_{n/2}(\overline{z z'}).
0767 | \end{align*}
0768 | \item (Real-complex case) If $x \in \R$ and $z \in \C_+$, then
0769 | \begin{align*}
0770 | \widetilde{S}_n(x,z) &:= \frac{i e^{-\frac{1}{2}(x-\overline{z})^2}}{\sqrt{2\pi}} \sqrt{\operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{2} \Im(z))} e^{-x\overline{z}} e_{n/2}(x\overline{z}) \\
0771 | \widetilde{S}_n(z,x) &:= \frac{e^{-\frac{1}{2}(x-z)^2}}{\sqrt{2\pi}} \sqrt{\operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{2} \Im(z))} e^{-xz} e_{n/2}(xz) + r_{n/2}(z,x) \\
0772 | \widetilde{DS}_n(x,z) &:= \frac{e^{-\frac{1}{2}(x-z)^2}}{\sqrt{2\pi}} (z-x) \sqrt{\operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{2} \Im(z))} e^{-xz} e_{n/2}(xz) \\
0773 | \widetilde{IS}_n(x,z) &:= -\frac{ie^{-\frac{1}{2}(x-\overline{z})^2}}{\sqrt{2\pi}} \sqrt{\operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{2} \Im(z))} e^{-x\overline{z}} e_{n/2}(x\overline{z}) - ir_{n/2}(\overline{z},x).
0774 | \end{align*}
0775 | \end{enumerate}
0776 | 
0777 | As ${\mathcal E}(\gamma,\gamma')$ is clearly bounded, it thus suffices (in view of \eqref{kt}) to show that all the expressions $\widetilde{S}_n(x,x')$, $\widetilde{DS}_n(x,x')$, $\widetilde{IS}_n(x,x')$, $\widetilde{S}_n(z,z')$, $\widetilde{DS}_n(z,z')$, $\widetilde{IS}_n(z,z')$, $\widetilde{S}_n(x,z)$, $\widetilde{S}_n(z,x)$, $\widetilde{DS}_n(x,z)$, $\widetilde{IS}_n(x,z)$ are all $O(1)$ for $x,x' \in \R$ and $z,z' \in \C_+$.  This will be a variant of the estimates in \cite[Section 9]{borodin}, which were concerned with the asymptotic values of these expressions as $n \to \infty$ rather than uniform bounds.
0778 | 
0779 | We first dispose of the $r_{n/2}$ terms.  In the proof of \cite[Corollary 9]{borodin}, the estimate
0780 | $$ |r_{n/2}(z,x)| \leq e^{-\frac{1}{2} \Re(z^2)} \sqrt{\operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{2}\Im(z))} \frac{|z|^{n-1}}{2^{n/2} (n/2 - 1)!}$$
0781 | is established for any $x \in \R$ and $z \in \C^+$.  Using the standard bound
0782 | \begin{equation}\label{erfc-bound}
0783 | \operatorname{erfc}(x) = O( \frac{e^{-x^2}}{1+x} )
0784 | \end{equation}
0785 | for any $x \geq 0$, we thus have
0786 | $$ |r_{n/2}(z,x)| \ll e^{-|z|^2/2} \frac{|z|^{n-1}}{2^{(n-1)/2} (n/2 - 1)!}.$$
0787 | But $\frac{|z|^{n-1}}{2^{(n-1)/2} (n/2 - 1)!}$ is one of the Taylor coefficients of $e^{|z|^2/2}$, and so
0788 | \begin{equation}\label{rhin}
0789 | r_{n/2}(z,x) = O(1).
0790 | \end{equation}
0791 | Thus we may ignore all terms involving $r_{n/2}$.
0792 | 
0793 | Now we handle the real-real case. Recall from the triangle inequality and Taylor expansion that
0794 | \begin{equation}\label{enz}
0795 |  |e_{n/2}(z)| \leq e_{n/2}(|z|) \leq \exp(|z|)
0796 | \end{equation}
0797 | for any complex number $z$.    Thus, for instance, we have
0798 | $$
0799 | |\widetilde{S}_n(x,x')| \ll \exp( - (x-x')^2/2 - xx' + |xx'| ) + 1 \ll 1$$
0800 | since the expression inside the exponential is either $-(x-x')^2/2$ or $-(x+x')^2/2$.
0801 | 
0802 | If one applies the same method to bound $\widetilde{DS}_n(x,x')$, one obtains
0803 | 
0804 | Similarly one has
0805 | $$
0806 | |\widetilde{DS}_n(x,x')| \ll |x-x'| \exp( - (x-x')^2/2 - xx' + |xx'| ).$$
0807 | This bound is $O(1)$ when $xx'$ is positive, but can grow linearly when $xx'$ is negative.  To deal with this issue, we need an alternate bound to \eqref{enz} that saves an additional polynomial factor in some cases:
0808 | 
0809 | \begin{lemma}[Alternate bound]\label{enz-alt}  For any complex number $z$, one has
0810 | $$ |e_{n/2}(z)| \ll \frac{|z|^{1/2}}{\left||z|-z\right|} \exp(|z|),$$
0811 | with the convention that the right-hand side is infinite when $z$ is a non-negative real.
0812 | \end{lemma}
0813 | 
0814 | \begin{proof}  The claim is trivial for $|z| \leq 1$, so we may assume that $|z| > 1$.  Observe that
0815 | \begin{equation}\label{zam}
0816 |  (|z|-z) e_{n/2}(z) = \sum_{m=0}^{n/2} \frac{z^m}{m!} (|z| - m) - \frac{z^{n/2+1}}{(n/2)!}.
0817 | \end{equation}
0818 | An application of Stirling's formula reveals that
0819 | $$ \frac{z^m}{m!} = O( \frac{1}{|z|^{1/2}} \exp(|z|) )$$
0820 | for all $m$, so the second term on the right-hand side of \eqref{zam} is $O( |z|  \frac{1}{|z|^{1/2}} \exp(|z|)  )$.  It thus suffices to show that
0821 | $$ \sum_{m=0}^{n/2} \frac{z^m}{m!} (|z| - m) = O(|z|^{1/2} \exp(|z|) ).$$
0822 | By the triangle inequality, the left-hand side can be bounded by
0823 | $$ \sum_{m \leq |z|} \frac{|z|^m}{m!} (|z|-m) + \sum_{m > |z|} \frac{|z|^m}{m!} (m-|z|).$$
0824 | This expression telescopes to
0825 | $$ 2 \frac{|z|^{m+1}}{m!} $$
0826 | where $m := \lfloor |z| \rfloor$. By Stirling's formula, this expression is $O( |z|^{1/2}  \exp(|z|) )$ as required.
0827 | \end{proof}
0828 | 
0829 | Inserting this bound in the case when $xx'$ is negative, we conclude that
